.he "nm (1)                       8/16/26                       nm (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
nm
- list the symbols in an object file
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
nm [ -bdgrv ] file ...
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Nm reads a Whitesmiths object file, or an archive of them, and lists
the symbol table.  Each symbol is one line: its value, the segment it
lives in, whether it is local or global, whether it is defined, and
its name.  With no option this is all it prints.
.sp
An archive is listed member by member, each under its own heading.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-b	Hex dump the text and data segments.

.ti -10
-d	Disassemble the text segment.

.ti -10
-g	Generate an assemblable .s file for the object, named after it.

.ti -10
-r	List the relocations.

.ti -10
-v	Show the header before the rest.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
This is not the BSD nm; it reads Whitesmiths object files, not a.out.
The name-list letters of the original are gone, and the flags have
other meanings: the original -g took only global symbols and -r
sorted in reverse, where here -g writes a .s file and -r lists
relocations.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ar (1), size (1)
